Title: The Last Shift
Credit: Written by
Author: [your name]
Draft date: September 2026
Notes: Original demo fixture. Deliberately exercises every breakdown category — cast, background, stunts, vehicles, animals, SFX, VFX, wardrobe, makeup, greenery, special equipment, sound, music, security, additional labor — across a night exterior, a company move, and two script days. Rewrite any line you like; it exists to be broken down, not admired.

====

INT. DELMAR DINER - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Fryer oil at a low boil. MARISOL VEGA, fifties, forearms scarred from twenty years of flat tops, scrapes carbon into a bus tub.

Through the pass, the dining room is empty except for a TRUCKER nursing coffee and two TEENAGERS splitting a plate of fries.

The walk-in door hangs open. Cold fog breathes across the floor.

MARISOL
Dale. The walk-in.

DALE OKONKWO (19), apron on backwards, shoulders the door shut.

DALE
It doesn't latch.

MARISOL
It latches if you lift it.

She lifts it. It latches.

DALE
Huh.

A SHEPHERD MIX shoves through the swinging door, leash dragging, snow melting on its back.

MARISOL
Absolutely not.

INT. DELMAR DINER - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

The dog has claimed booth four. The Truckers coat hangs on the hook beside it.

The TRUCKER doesn't look up from his coffee.

TRUCKER
He rides with me.

MARISOL
There's a sign.

TRUCKER
There's a lot of signs.

Marisol sets a saucer of water on the floor. She does not acknowledge doing it.

Above the register, a small TELEVISION plays a weather map. The whole northern county is one solid red mass, crawling east.

DALE (O.S.)
Marisol.

MARISOL
What.

DALE (O.S.)
Fire.

INT. DELMAR DINER - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

A grease fire climbs the back wall of the flat top. Two feet. Then four.

Dale reaches for the sink sprayer. Marisol catches his wrist hard enough to turn him.

MARISOL
Water spreads it.

She drags the extinguisher off its bracket, pulls the pin, and puts the fire down in six seconds flat. White powder settles over everything: the flat top, the prep counter, her hair.

Silence. The hood fan whines.

DALE
I was going to --

MARISOL
I know what you were going to do.

She hands him the extinguisher.

MARISOL
Now you know something.

EXT. STATE ROUTE 9 - SHOULDER - NIGHT

Snow coming sideways. Marisol's PICKUP TRUCK sits at an angle on the shoulder, hazards ticking, one wheel over the lip of the drainage ditch.

She stands in the headlights in a diner apron and a parka, hood down, staring at the wheel.

Behind her, a SEMI passes close enough to move her.

STUNT NOTE: Truck slides on the shoulder and stops with the front wheel over the ditch edge. Precision driver. Ditch dressed with breakaway brush.

The Trucker's rig pulls in ahead of her, air brakes hissing. He climbs down with a tow strap over one shoulder. The dog watches from his cab.

TRUCKER
Lift it and it latches?

MARISOL
Something like that.

EXT. DELMAR DINER - PARKING LOT - DAWN

Script Day 2.

The storm has stopped. The lot is a clean unbroken white, except for one set of tire tracks pulling out and away toward the highway.

The diner's neon sign flickers on: DELMAR. The M is dead.

Marisol locks the front door, tries it, and stands there a moment with her hand flat against the glass.

She walks to her truck. It starts on the second try.

FADE OUT.
